what's up everybody welcome back to my  youtube channel richard on data if this  is your first time here my name is  Richard and this is the channel where we  talk about all things data data science  statistics and programming subscribe for  all kinds of content just like this and  at the notification bell so YouTube  notifies you whenever I upload a video  I'm gonna talk about a concept that all  too often gets forgotten when people  interpret results in the fields of  applied statistics and data science and  that effect is called regression to the  mean  also known as the regression effect this  has some pretty major implications in  those instances when you're doing  subsequent or even repeated measurements  over time and if you're unaware of it  you can come away with some pretty  serious misinterpretations so I'm going  to give you a definition of regression  to the mean then I'm going to explain  why it's true and then we're gonna go  through an overview of some of its  implications here's the definition so  let's suppose somebody takes two  distinct measurements and for sake of an  example let's just say somebody's taking  a test then they take a subsequent  follow-up test but it could be anything  and then we observe that the first  measurement is somewhat extreme either  high or low that individual is likely on  average to be closer to the mean on the  subsequent tests now this is just a  statistical tendency it's not guaranteed  to happen but especially as you start to  take repeated measurements this becomes  more and more likely to occur  now you're hopefully asking yourself why  this is the case so I'm gonna walk  through three different ways in which  regression to the mean can be  demonstrated the first way to  demonstrate regression to the mean is  what I like to call the all chance  versus no chance scenarios and if we  come back to our test score example I  think almost everybody intuitively would  agree that the measured score is equal  to your ability Plus chance now let's  think through two different extreme  scenarios if the measured score came  down 100% to look our chance or error or  whatever you want to call that or if  there's no luck or chance involved  whatsoever let's think about the  z-scores here so if it all came down to  chance  then we would expect the z-score of the  measurement to be equal to zero right I  mean that's the expected value of a  normal 0 1 distribution and we're  assuming here that the chance isn't  biased in one direction or the other  that is lucky versus unlucky being more  likely than the other if none of it had  to do with chance then every measurement  would be exactly the same the  measurement would be exactly equal to  ability and so we would get that same  score each and every single time  turns out reality it's somewhere in the  middle there's always going to be some  ability and some chance that comes into  play  so our z-score is expected to be  somewhere between those two extremes  it's expected to be between a z-score of  0 that is the mean and the z-score from  the previous measurement now on the  threat of z-scores let's put this in  another way and let's just assume we  have a formula Z 2 equals Z 1 plus  chance Z 1 is the z-score of the first  measurement Z 2 is the z-score of the  second measurement it's a statistical  rule that have two variables let's call  them x and y are independent then the  variance of X plus y is equal to the  variance of X plus the variance of Y if  we apply that rule here we would end up  with the variance of Z 2 is equal to the  variance of Z 1 plus the variance due to  chance and we've agreed that the  variance due to chance is greater than  zero so one of two things has to be true  here either the variance of Z 2 is  greater than the variance of Z 1 or our  whole original premise was off turns out  it's the latter it turns out that for  the variance of Z 1 and Z 2 to be equal  and for the variance of sums rule to  hold true here Z 2 has to equal C times  Z 1 plus chance where C is some constant  between 0 &amp; 1 as a result the expected  value of Z 2 equals C times the expected  value of Z 1 as a result the expected  value of the z-score is going to be  closer to 0 meaning the measurement is  expected to be closer to the mean than  the first one now another way to  demonstrate regression to the  it's through the simple linear  regression equation let's consult our  good friend Wikipedia for this one so we  start from the simple linear regression  equation y equals alpha plus beta times  X where alpha is the intercept term and  beta is the slope term and we obviously  have the well-established least squares  estimates alpha hat and beta hat beta  hat is the correlation coefficient times  the standard deviation of Y over the  standard deviation of X alpha hat equals  y bar minus beta hat times X bar where Y  bar and X bar are the sample means of Y  and X respectively  so now just plug alpha hat and beta hat  into the equation for y to arrive at the  fitted value y hat and yes you have to  plug the expression for beta hat into  the expression for alpha hat if you're  following along but it's all  straightforward algebra you end up  arriving at the equation y hat minus y  bar over the standard deviation of y  equals the correlation coefficient times  X minus X bar over the standard  deviation of X now unless you have  perfect linearity the correlation  coefficient is somewhere between  negative 1 and 1 so do those of you who  are new to this what does all that mean  well it means that the predicted value  of y given a regression on some variable  X is expected to be closer to the mean  of Y then X is to the mean of X now this  won't happen if you have perfect  linearity and R is equal to negative 1  or 1 but in the real world that never  happens and in fact the closer R is to 0  the more regression to the mean occurs  and now that hopefully I've convinced  you that regression to the mean is a  very real thing let's turn back to  Wikipedia for some examples of it and  there are examples of it in literally  every domain that you could think of  back to the example of people taking  tests so let's suppose you have people  taking two versions of a test on two  successive days what you're probably  going to see is that most of the people  who did the worst on day one do better  on day two and the people who did the  best on day one  do worse on day two and think of this in  terms of the first explanation I gave  where a score is based somewhat on  ability and somewhat unchaste a lot of  people who did really well at first did  so out of luck and so that's not  necessarily likely to play out the  second time around and similarly people  who did worse than their ability the  first time around will tend to see their  scores increase the second time that is  to be closer to the mean the second time  around for the same general reason and  there's plenty of other examples so  let's say we have a sports team and they  win a championship now obviously they  aren't guaranteed to win the second time  around and in fact the more of their  first success that was just due to luck  the less likely that they'll win again  next year although of course the flip  side of that is true - and the more of  the wind this year that can be  attributed to skill the better their  chances are next year similarly in  clinical trials a drug that outperformed  other candidate drugs is less likely -  if we do a second trial than a business  that had a highly well performing  quarter is expected to do more average  the next quarter unless there was an  underlying reason for doing better the  first time but the last example here is  actually the most telling one in my  opinion  so baseball players with great batting  averages tend to get cover features on  magazines then they do less well over  the next period of time that their  performance gets tracked thus leading to  this theory that appearing on the cover  of magazines causes them to perform less  well when no that's generally not it at  all it's just regression to the mean and  that's really the biggest takeaway here  we all want to attribute improvements or  declines due to something causing that  improvement or decline when in reality  we would expect to see some of these  things naturally just because of  regression to the mean this is something  any applied statistician or data  scientist needs to keep in mind because  if you're unaware of this you're likely  to make some serious interpretation  errors  and fall back into your own preconceived  notions and you should always ask  yourself this question can we rule out  that whatever we're observing is just  due to the regression effect if not you  may want to think about collecting more  data before you make too strong of a  conclusion so thanks for watching this  video if you enjoyed it and you would  like to support my work the most helpful  thing that you could do for me would be  to share this video otherwise at least  consider smashing the like button and  then leave a comment down below and let  me know when you've run to regression to  the mean then I'll see you all in the  not so distant future until then Richard  on data

